    written: [
      { q: "Name the parents of Shivaji.", a: "Shivaji's father was Shahaji Bhonsle and his mother was Jijabai.", marks: 1, type: "short" },
      { q: "What was the Ashtapradhan?", a: "It was the council of eight ministers who assisted Shivaji in governing his kingdom.", marks: 1, type: "short" },
      { q: "Distinguish between chauth and sardeshmukhi in their use by the Marathas.", a: "Both were taxes levied on neighbouring lands, not on Shivaji's own territory.\nChauth was one-fourth of the revenue of those lands; with sardeshmukhi, an extra levy, they added to the state's income.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Mention two features of Shivaji's military organisation.", a: "He kept a disciplined army and also built a navy.\nHe built and garrisoned many hill forts and used guerrilla tactics in the Western Ghats.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe Shivaji's struggle against Bijapur and the Mughals.", a: "(i) He began by capturing Torna in 1646 and other forts, and in 1659 killed the Bijapur general Afzal Khan at Pratapgarh.\n(ii) By the Treaty of Purandar (1665) he gave up many forts to the Mughals under Jai Singh.\n(iii) He visited Aurangzeb at Agra in 1666 and escaped from confinement, later being crowned Chhatrapati in 1674.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the role of the Peshwas in Maratha history.", a: "(i) Under Shahu, real power passed to the Peshwas, the chief ministers at Pune.\n(ii) Balaji Vishwanath, Baji Rao I and Balaji Baji Rao led Maratha expansion across central and northern India.\n(iii) The office became hereditary in practice, and the Peshwas ruled in the name of the Chhatrapati.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What were the results of the Third Battle of Panipat (1761)?", a: "(i) Ahmad Shah Abdali defeated the Marathas and they suffered heavy losses of leaders and soldiers.\n(ii) Their hope of controlling Delhi and northern India was ended.\n(iii) The Marathas stayed powerful in the south and west but were weakened before the advance of the British.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the administration of Shivaji.", a: "(i) Shivaji was assisted by the Ashtapradhan, a council of eight ministers.\n(ii) Income came from land revenue and from chauth and sardeshmukhi collected from neighbouring lands.\n(iii) He kept a disciplined army and a navy.\n(iv) Many hill forts were built and strongly garrisoned.\n(v) He stressed efficient revenue collection, which provided a strong base for the new state.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Trace the rise of the Maratha power from Shivaji to 1761.", a: "(i) Shivaji captured Torna in 1646 and built a kingdom in the Western Ghats; he killed Afzal Khan in 1659.\n(ii) He was crowned Chhatrapati at Raigad in 1674 and died in 1680.\n(iii) Sambhaji was executed by Aurangzeb in 1689, but Rajaram and Tarabai carried on the war.\n(iv) Under Shahu, the Peshwas at Pune (Balaji Vishwanath, Baji Rao I, Balaji Baji Rao) spread Maratha power across central and northern India.\n(v) It received a heavy blow at the Third Battle of Panipat in 1761 against Ahmad Shah Abdali.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A history student prepares a timeline. In 1646 a young leader captures a hill fort called Torna. In 1659 he kills the Bijapur general at Pratapgarh. In 1674 he is crowned at Raigad with the title Chhatrapati. He rules with a council of eight ministers and collects extra taxes from neighbouring lands.\n(i) Who is this leader?\n(ii) Name the council of ministers.\n(iii) Name the two extra taxes he collected from neighbouring lands.", a: "(i) Shivaji.\n(ii) The Ashtapradhan.\n(iii) Chauth and sardeshmukhi.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
